Asteroid geometry variants:
```js
function rockGeo(radius, detail){
  const g = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(radius, detail);
  const p = g.attributes.position;
  const v = new THREE.Vector3();
  for(let i=0;i<p.count;i++){
    v.set(p.getX(i),p.getY(i),p.getZ(i));
    const s = 1 + (Math.random()-0.5)*0.5; // careful: shared vertices get displaced differently creating cracks
  }
}
```

IcosahedronGeometry in r128 is non-indexed? BufferGeometry from IcosahedronGeometry (PolyhedronGeometry) is non-indexed in newer versions; in r128... PolyhedronBufferGeometry produces non-indexed triangles (each face separate vertices). Displacing per-vertex randomly would tear the mesh (cracks). Solution: displace deterministically by hashing the vertex position so identical positions displace identically: use a pseudo-noise based on direction: `const n = 0.72 + 0.55*noiseish(v.normalize())`. Use a cheap hash: 

```js
function hash3(x,y,z){ return Math.sin(x*12.9898 + y*78.233 + z*37.719)*43758.5453 % 1; }
```
Take fract. That's deterministic per position → no cracks.

```js
function rockGeo(radius, detail){
  const g = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(radius, detail);
  const p = g.attributes.position;
  const v = new THREE.Vector3();
  for(let i=0;i<p.count;i++){
    v.set(p.getX(i),p.getY(i),p.getZ(i)).normalize();
    const h = Math.sin(v.x*5.3+SEED)*Math.cos(v.y*4.7+SEED*1.3)*Math.sin(v.z*6.1+SEED*0.7);
    const s = 1 + h*0.36;
    p.setXYZ(i, v.x*radius*s, v.y*radius*s, v.z*radius*s);
  }
  g.computeVertexNormals();
  return g;
}
```
Hmm but vertices at same position normalize to same direction → same displacement. Good, no cracks. Use different seed per variant. scale: `1 + 0.28*noise` where noise in [-1,1].

Actually I want lumpy: multiply radius by (0.75..1.3). With h in [-1,1], s = 1+h*0.3 → [0.7,1.3]. Good.

Variants: for each size class create 4 geometries. Sizes: LARGE r=11 detail 1, MED r=6.2 detail 1, SMALL r=3.2 detail 1? detail 1 = 80 faces, plenty. Actually icosahedron detail 1 has 80 faces; detail 0 has 20 — chunky and stylized. Use detail 1 for large, 1 for med, 0 for small? Mixed looks fine.

Materials: MeshStandardMaterial({color:0x8a7a6a, roughness:0.9, metalness:0.05, flatShading:true}). A couple color variants: 0x8a7a6a (brown-gray), 0x6f7a86 (blue-gray), 0x7a6a5a.

Asteroid object:
```js
function spawnAsteroid(size, x, z, vx, vz){
  const geo = rockGeos[size][rand int];
  const mat = rockMats[rand];
  const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geo, mat);
  mesh.position.set(x, 0, z);
  const r = ROCK_R[size]; // effective collision radius ~ radius*avg
  const a = { mesh, size, r, vel:new THREE.Vector3(vx,0,vz), rot:new THREE.Vector3(rand,rand,rand) };
  asteroids.push(a); scene.add(mesh);
}
```

Radius per size: LARGE 12 (geometry radius 11 * lumps up to 1.3 → 14.3 max; use collision r = 11*1.05≈11.5 to be forgiving-ish but visually mostly ok), MED 6.5, SMALL 3.4. Slightly smaller than visual max = forgiving = good.

Spawn initial field: 6-8 large asteroids at random positions at least 90 units from center (ship spawn), random velocity 8-18 u/s, directions random. Plus continuous top-up: if asteroids.length < targetCount, spawn every few seconds at arena edge (away from ship) as large or medium. Difficulty: targetCount = 6 + floor(score/800), capped 14.

Points: LARGE 20, MED 50, SMALL 100. Score popup? skip.

